ree hearts — possible if a player holds two hearts.\n2. **Three of a kind (trips 6s)**: if someone holds 6-x.\n3. **Two pair**: e.g., 9-7, 9-4, 7-4, etc.\n4. **One pair (6s)** with good kicker — which is what you have.\n\nNow, game history:\n- Pre-flop: Opponent raised 200 → you called (action 0).\n- Flop: Opponent raised 100 → you raised to 200 → opponent called.\n- Turn: Opponent checked → you checked.\n- River: Opponent checked → now it's your turn.\n\nSo current round (River): opponent checked. You are to act after the river.\n\nYour valid actions: [-2, -1, 0, 100, or >100 up to your chip stack].\n\nYou have 19,550 chips.\n\nSince opponent checked on the river, they may be:\n- Weak, checking to induce a bet.\n- Or strong, trapping.\n\nBut your hand is **marginal**: one pair, medium kicker (King). But **many better hands possible**.\n\nHowever, you **bluffed aggressively on the flop** — you re-raised after opponent’s bet, showing strength.\n\nNow, on the river, after both checked turn and opponent checked river, you can:\n- Check back (0): show down weak-medium hand.\n- Bet (e.g., 100 or more): for value or bluff.\n- Fold (-1): only if you think you’re beaten always — but no reason to fold a pair.\n- All-in (-2): extreme overbet.\n\nBut you should consider:\n- Did your aggression on flop represent a strong hand (like flush draw, set, two pair)?\n- Now, on river, if you bet, you continue that story.\n- But board has three hearts — if opponent has a heart, they may have hit flush.\n\nYou have K♥ — so you block the nut heart flush draw (A♥ would be bigger, but K♥ is second nuts if flush were out).\n\nBut no flush is possible unless someone has two hearts — and with K♥ in your hand, fewer combos.\n\nStill, opponent may have Q♥J♥, A♥x♥, etc.\n\nBut if they had flush, why check river? Maybe they missed.\n\nAlternatively, they may have a weaker one-pair hand and are giving up.\n\nYour best move:\n- **Small bet for value** — say 100 — to extract from worse one-pair hands